ap values and real world deployment concerns like how to export model runtime stability or hardware constraint. These kind of issues were handled after the model was already released. But we all know that in real applications deployment is the hardest part. High accuracy alone does not solve real deployment problems. So this was the problem statement which Ultralytics focused on while building this YOLO 26. And the main goal of YOLO 26 is to make the object detection efficient, practical and reliable on edge devices and on low power devices. And to achieve this, YOLO 26 introduced several design level and several training level optimizations. Now we will discuss all of them one by one. First let's see the paper. Let me show you what all changes they have mentioned and then we will discuss them. So just scroll down and we will go to the section where they have mentioned this architectural enhancements. So they have mentioned few improvements in this section. Let's discuss that. So the first improvement is removal of distribution focal loss. What it is? Why they did changes in it? That part we will discuss in some time. And the second change is improvement is end to end NMS free inference. And after that the next change is this progressive loss and this S t. So this part also we are going to discuss in some time. And then the fourth change is this where they combined two optimizers. So these are the four things which we are going to discuss now and these are the four major improvements which they introduced in their paper. So let's start with this end to end NMS free inference. This improvement is basically related to how duplicate predictions are handled and if you have worked with object detection then most probably you already know about this NMS non-max suppression. So in earlier YOLO models, the model used to predict many overlapping bounding boxes for objects and then a separate post-processing step called NMS was used to remove the duplicate boxes and NMS works well but the problem is extra computation, extra complexity and unpredictable inference time because for every image NMS has to process so many boxes which is computationally expensive and especially on edge devices. So, YOLO 26 stopped using this NMS process. Means instead of fixing the overlapping bounding boxes or duplicate bounding boxes after the prediction, the model is trained to avoid producing too many duplicate boxes, too many duplicate bounding boxes in the first place. And for that, YOLO 26 learns during training only that which predictions should survive and which predictions should be suppressed. And if this step is removed then automatically inference become faster which is extremely important for real time and edge development. Now another major improvement is that YOLO 26 completely moves away from anchor boxes. And guys if you remember in older YOLO versions anchor boxes were used as a starting point. So anchor boxes are predefined rectangles of different shapes and sizes. And these anchor boxes had to be manually designed. And if the anchor boxes did not match with the object sizes which we have used in our data set then performance could drop. So that's why YOLO 26 use a fully anchor-free approach means instead of starting from predefined box shapes the model uh directly predicts the object location and size and this simplifies the training process. This makes the model more robust across different data sets because earlier you need to take care that the your data set the objects which are there in your data set should uh you know wor